<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Santa Fe de Nuevo México</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is a region of the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, today a US state. Spanish colonisation began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> in 1598, and the province, centred on Santa Fe from about 1610, was the northern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680 drove the Spaniards out until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> returned in 1692. It passed to Mexico in 1821 and to the United States in 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-mexico.jpg" alt="New Mexico" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Mexico.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is a landlocked state in the south-west, sharing the Four Corners region with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> and its valley run north to south through the centre. The Sangre de Cristo Mountains lie east of the river in the north, the Great Plains reach into the eastern third, the Colorado Plateau dominates the north-west, and the Chihuahuan Desert extends through the south. The landscape is more varied than its popular image as arid desert.</p><p>The name was given long before present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> took it in 1821. After the Spanish conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> in the early 16th century, they called the region they began to explore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Nuevo México</a>, after the Kingdom of Mexico. In 1581 the Chamuscado and Rodríguez Expedition named the area north of the Rio Grande San Felipe del Nuevo México. The Spaniards had hoped to find wealthy cultures like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, but the cultures proved unrelated and lacking in riches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known inhabitants were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleo-indians">Paleo-Indians</a>. Later came the Mogollon and the Ancestral Pueblo cultures, known for sophisticated pottery and urban development, with pueblos or remains at Acoma, Taos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Culture National Historical Park</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> arrived in the late 1400s.</p><p>Esteban de Dorantes was the first person from the Old World to reach New Mexico, in 1539, and was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> y Salazar began Spanish colonisation in 1598. In the Ácoma Massacre of 1599 his army set fire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo">Acoma Pueblo</a> and approximately 800 to 1,000 Ácoma were killed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> then ordered that the right foot of every man over 25 be cut off. Santa Fe was founded in 1610 as the government seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Nuevo México</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680 was the first successful revolution against colonial rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Spanish rule returned in 1692. After Mexican independence in 1821 the province became an autonomous territory, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexed it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> in 1848. New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was a territory for 64 years and became the 47th state on 6 January 1912. It hosted key facilities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a> and was the site of the first nuclear test, Trinity, in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Santa Fe is the capital, the oldest state capital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a> is the largest city. The state is home to one-third of the Navajo Nation, 19 federally recognised Pueblo communities and three federally recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> tribes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Mexico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At about that time some of these bands, allied with the Utes, moved south-east towards the southern Plains, where bison were plentiful and horses, spreading from <b>New Mexico</b> after the Pueblo Revolt of 1680, could be had by trade and raiding.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On the northern frontier, a line of presidios, or garrisoned forts, protected missions and mining towns against Apache and Comanche raids, and the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 drove the Spaniards out of <b>New Mexico</b> until Diego de Vargas returned in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lamar's unofficial Santa Fe Expedition of 1841, 320 soldiers with merchants, aimed at eastern <b>New Mexico</b>; its members were captured, and two raids on the Santa Fe Trail in 1843 also failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ancestral Puebloans are the ancestors of the Pueblo peoples of Arizona and <b>New Mexico</b>: the Hopi, the Zuni, the people of Acoma and Laguna and the pueblos of the Rio Grande.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>1 near Clovis, <b>New Mexico</b>, where stone tools were found with the remains of Columbian mammoths in 1929.</blockquote><a href="/culture/clovis-culture">Clovis culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Frybread, created when the Navajo were forced to relocate to <b>New Mexico</b>, is regarded as a symbol of Native American perseverance.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
